<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Castel Sant'Angelo</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">2nd century</span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century">2nd century</a> · Also known as Mausoleum of Hadrian</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Castel Sant'Angelo is a fortress in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> on the right bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiber-river">Tiber</a>, built in the second century as the mausoleum of the emperor Hadrian. Converted into a fortress in late antiquity, it was linked to the Vatican by a fortified corridor and served the popes as a citadel, prison and refuge, notably for Clement VII during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sack-of-rome-1527">Sack of Rome</a> in 1527. It is now a museum.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/castel-santangelo.jpg" alt="Castel Sant'Angelo" width="320" height="287" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Livioandronico2013 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Castel_Sant%27Angelo_at_Night.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
